# HITE environment. Copy to .env.local and fill in.
# Every variable below is read by real code (verified by grepping `process.env` across the repo).
# Nothing here is aspirational — if a variable is listed, something reads it.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED — the app cannot serve its core loop without these
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Supabase project (auth + database + STORAGE). Create a project at https://supabase.com,
# then apply supabase/migrations/*.sql — migration 005 creates the `media`, `exports` and
# `previews` buckets and their policies, so uploads need no extra provisioning and no extra
# variable. Read by lib/supabase/{client,server,admin}.ts, lib/storage/* and middleware.ts.
# The ANON key is public by design (it ships in the browser bundle, and the browser uploads
# directly to Storage with it); your data is protected by Row Level Security, not by hiding
# this key.
#
# ⚠ ANONYMOUS SIGN-INS MUST BE ENABLED ON THIS PROJECT. HITE has no sign-in screen — middleware.ts
# mints a Supabase anonymous session on the first request to /app, which is what gives RLS an
# `auth.uid()` to scope every row and every `{userId}/…` storage prefix by. With it off, /app
# answers 503 for everyone; there is no login page to fall back to.
#   local:  `enable_anonymous_sign_ins = true` in supabase/config.toml (already set), then restart
#   hosted: Dashboard → Authentication → Sign In / Providers → Anonymous
#
# ⚠ WHAT THAT COSTS YOU ON A PUBLIC DEPLOYMENT, STATED PLAINLY: anyone who loads your URL becomes a
# user, can create projects, upload footage into your Storage, and spend YOUR Gemini quota.
#   · What still holds: they cannot read anyone else's data. RLS is per-user and is proven at
#     runtime for ANONYMOUS sessions specifically, in tests/integration/rls.test.ts.
#   · What does NOT hold the way it used to: the daily caps in migration 003 (plan 10 / refine 30 /
#     render 10, lib/cache/jobs.ts) are keyed by user id, and a visitor who clears cookies is a NEW
#     user with a fresh allowance. Per-user caps bound an ordinary visitor, not a determined one.
#     Migration 004's per-IP cap only covers POST /api/errors, so it does not help here.
#   · The one real brake on mass account creation is Supabase's own per-IP anonymous sign-in limit
#     ([auth.rate_limit] anonymous_users, 30/hour by default).
# If your deployment is public and you do not want to fund strangers, put it behind your own network
# gate (Vercel password protection, an access proxy, a private URL). HITE does not ship one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Service-role key — bypasses RLS. SERVER ONLY, never expose to the client.
# Read by lib/supabase/admin.ts for background/analysis writes and for the render worker's
# upload into the private `exports` bucket.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THERE IS NO MODEL KEY HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
#
# HITE is BRING-YOUR-OWN-KEY ONLY. Every AI edit runs on the USER'S own provider key, which arrives
# per request in the `x-hite-provider-key` header, is held in their browser, and is never written to
# the database, a cache or a log. There is no deployer key pool, so a keyless request cannot quietly
# spend your money: it gets a 402 pointing at the settings screen.
#
# Users choose among eight providers (Google, OpenAI, Anthropic, Groq, xAI, DeepSeek, OpenRouter, and
# a self-hosted OpenAI-compatible endpoint) — see lib/ai/providers/registry.ts.
#
# Be straight with your users about the part that is unavoidable: your server sees their key in
# transit, because something has to call the provider. The UI says so before the input; do not remove
# that line (BYOK_DISCLOSURE in lib/ai/keys.ts).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SPEECH — unset costs you a whole category of edit, not a degraded version of one
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Groq (Whisper Turbo) — the ONLY transcription path in this build. Read by worker/transcribe.ts,
# which fails the transcribe branch loudly when it is unset rather than storing an empty transcript.
#
# WITHOUT IT THERE IS NO TRANSCRIPT, AND EVERYTHING DERIVED FROM ONE IS GONE, NOT DEGRADED:
#   · captions and any edit that places words on screen
#   · "cut the dead air" / silence trimming — `findSilences` measures gaps BETWEEN transcript
#     segments, so with no transcript it reports `transcribed: false` and finds nothing at all.
#     Nothing else in the pipeline detects silence, so there is no second-best answer here.
# The rest is unaffected and each analysis branch persists on its own: cuts, looks, effects, beats,
# scene detection and export all work with this unset.
GROQ_API_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL — features degrade gracefully when unset
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Canonical origin used for metadata, JSON-LD, sitemap and robots.
# Defaults to the production domain; set this for local or self-hosted deployments
# so canonical URLs don't point at someone else's site. Read by lib/seo/jsonld.ts.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Whether this build serves the editor, which decides what the primary CTA is allowed to promise:
#   local           → "Open the editor" → /app        (a cloned repo; the editor is right here)
#   hosted-landing  → "Get it running"  → #quickstart (www.tryhite.xyz; the editor is not deployed)
# YOU DO NOT NORMALLY SET THIS. next.config.ts derives it — builds on Vercel get "hosted-landing",
# builds anywhere else get "local" — precisely so nobody has to remember, because the failure mode is
# a landing page telling the whole internet to open an editor that is not there. Set it only if you
# genuinely deploy the editor somewhere. Read by lib/site/primaryCta.ts.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_HITE_EDITOR=local

# Default Gemini model when a request names a provider but no model. Advanced; the user's own
# selection wins. There is deliberately no per-effort-level model override any more: the ladder picks
# the SHAPE of a turn and the USER picks the model.
# HITE_GEMINI_MODEL=

# Self-hosted OpenAI-compatible provider (Ollama, LM Studio, vLLM, llama.cpp, LiteLLM).
# OFF by default and it must stay off on a public deployment: the user supplies a base URL that YOUR
# SERVER fetches, which is an SSRF pivot, and on a hosted server `localhost` is the server's own
# localhost anyway. Even when enabled the URL is validated to a loopback host before any request.
# HITE_ALLOW_LOCAL_PROVIDER=1

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EFFORT + ABUSE BRAKES (self-hosting on a public URL)
# Read by lib/ai/effort.ts and lib/api/rate-limit.ts. Every one is optional; the defaults are what
# www.tryhite.xyz runs. Note what these protect: your SERVER's compute. They are not spend caps — the
# user spends their own money, so capping it would be pure friction that protects nothing.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# How far up the effort ladder (draft | standard | high | max) a run may go. Higher levels cost the
# USER materially more tokens and hold YOUR serverless function materially longer, so this is a
# function-seconds brake, not a spend cap. Unset means no brake; the real ceiling is then whatever
# the user's chosen provider can honestly serve (lib/ai/effort.ts `rungCeiling`) — a provider that
# does not enforce `tool_choice` is capped at `standard`, because a grounded rung on such a provider
# ends the turn with no edit at all.
# HITE_BYOK_EFFORT_CEILING=max

# NOT WIRED UP YET — setting this does nothing today. Read before you rely on it.
#
# The intent: the user pays their own provider, but YOU pay for the function-seconds a long run
# holds, so BYOK would trade a daily count cap for a per-IP cap on how many planner runs may be IN
# FLIGHT at once. `requirePlannerBudget` (lib/api/rate-limit.ts) implements it and
# supabase/migrations/008_byok_runs.sql provides the RPC — but NO ROUTE CALLS IT. /api/plan and
# /api/refine do not take the budget, so there is no concurrency ceiling and nothing fails closed.
#
# This is documented rather than deleted because the mechanism is written and tested; what is missing
# is the call site. Until a route takes the budget, a self-hosted deployment has NO protection here
# and should put Vercel WAF rate-limiting (or equivalent) in front. IP would be a cost ceiling and
# never an identity in any case: CGNAT and proxies blur it.
# HITE_BYOK_MAX_CONCURRENT=2

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TESTS — only needed to run the integration suites against a local Supabase stack
# (`supabase start`). Without them, tests/integration/*.test.ts SKIP — and the database
# and storage policies go unproven. See tests/integration/README.md.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SUPABASE_LOCAL_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54421
# SUPABASE_LOCAL_ANON_KEY=
# SUPABASE_LOCAL_SERVICE_KEY=

# Provider verification harness (scripts/verify-provider.ts) — a DEVELOPER/TEST convenience, never a
# product path. It reads the key for the provider under test from HITE_VERIFY_KEY; for Google it also
# accepts the first entry of GEMINI_API_KEYS. Nothing in a request path reads either variable.
# HITE_VERIFY_KEY=
# GEMINI_API_KEYS=

# End-to-end smoke script (scripts/smoke.ts) and the Playwright QA suite.
# SMOKE_URL=http://localhost:3000
# SMOKE_TOKEN=
# QA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000
